course: dictionary
lesson: 13771
title: Intervening: the word in between
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Intervening» is an adjective when it means coming or lying between two things, places or times. Read this: «In the intervening weeks, Sam practised every day.» The clue is that there are two events, and the weeks sit between them, so intervening must mean the weeks in the middle.
explain: «The intervening hills blocked our view of the lake.» Ask yourself: what is in the way? The hills stand between you and the lake. Swap your meaning into the sentence: «The hills lying between us blocked our view.» It still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Intervening is also a form of the verb intervene, which means stepping in to stop or change something. «When the boys began to fight, the teacher stepped in, intervening to stop it.» The clue is the fight: someone stepped in to stop it, so intervening means stepping in.
fact: Intervening is an adjective when it means coming or lying between two things, places or times.
fact: Common phrases are: the intervening time, the intervening period and the intervening years.
fact: As a verb form, intervening means stepping in to stop or change something.
fact: The verb forms are intervene, intervenes, intervened and intervening.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «The intervening hills blocked our view of the lake.» What does intervening mean here? | Very tall and covered in snow | Moving quickly down the valley | Shining brightly in the sun | *Lying between us and the lake
exercise: «In the intervening years, the little village became a busy town.» What does intervening mean? | Very old and falling down | Sudden and surprising | *Coming between two times | Happening in the future
exercise: «When the boys began to fight, the teacher stepped in, intervening to stop it.» What does intervening mean in this sentence? | Cheering loudly for one side | *Stepping in to stop or change something | Watching from far away | Running away from the fight
test: «In the intervening weeks, Sam practised every day.» What do the intervening weeks mean? | The weeks that came before anything else | The weeks when Sam was ill | The coldest weeks of the year | *The weeks that came between two events
test: «The intervening trees hid the house from view.» Which meaning fits best? | Trees that were very old | Trees covered in bright red leaves | *Trees standing between us and the house | Trees that had fallen down
exam: «Mum saw the cousins arguing, so she went over, intervening before anything broke.» What does intervening mean here? | Lying between two houses | Waiting for the cousins to leave | Sitting down to rest | *Stepping in to stop something
